' BASIC commands supported:

' Suffix-less variables are supported, and implicitly typed dynamic strings are permitted
' Variable names have a 64 character limit
' Add GRAPHICS at the beginning for a graphics window (640 x 480 default)
' PSET to draw a single dot to the framebuffer - PSET X, Y, color

' The compiler itself is a polling-based batch processor, and the graphical executable programs it generates are event-driven

' NOTE: TYPING MECHANICS
' At the core memory-resolution level, the backend uses compile-time type inference
' (Top-Down Implicit Typing / RHS Lookahead) to resolve suffix-less variables
' The compiler embraces modern QB64-style tolerance: if a suffix-less variable
' is assigned a string (e.g., MyVar = "Hello"), the RHS lookahead implicitly
' types it as a dynamic string seamlessly at compile time.

' NOTE: STRING DECLARATIONS (STRINGD vs STRING) AND #STRINGSTRICT
' While implicit typing handles implicitly declared strings cleanly, explicit declarations are managed
' based on the #STRINGSTRICT directive:
' When #STRINGSTRICT is active, strict separation is enforced:
'  - STRINGD : Explicit dynamic string. Cannot have a * length.
'  - STRING  : Fixed-length string. Requires a * and a numeric length. Permanently
'              preserves whatever suffix state ($ or no $) was used at declaration
' When #STRINGSTRICT is not active, the compiler behaves like standard QB64:
'  - STRING  : Can act as a dynamic string (AS STRING) or fixed-length (AS STRING * len)
'  - STRINGD : Always acts as a dynamic string and cannot have a * length
' Strings implicitly defined on the fly will always default to dynamic

' NOTE: TIRA (THREE-ADDRESS CODE) NAMESPACE PREFIXES
' The TIRA intermediate representation relies on a strict prefix system to define
' the nature of variables and labels during the compilation phase:
'  !G_ (exclamation) : Persistent global compiler/runtime state and data symbols
'                      (e.g., !G_WND_VAL_HWND).
'  !C_ (exclamation) : Compiler-generated constructs, constants, and literal symbols
'                      (e.g., !C_EVL_DSC_LTRL_12).
'  !R_ (exclamation) : Ephemeral variables belonging to emitted runtime helpers.
'                      tiraDimVar$ applies this prefix automatically while isCompilingRT is set.
'  !T  (exclamation) : Ephemeral variables used while lowering ordinary program code.
'                      These are strictly internal and dynamically typed/allocated.
'
'  @   (at)          : Compiler-internal addresses and jump labels. Fixed internal anchors
'                      may use direct names such as @END_PROGRAM. Ordinary generated labels
'                      retain the @BASE_counter form (e.g., @SKIP_CASE_12).
'  @R_ (at)          : Generated labels belonging to emitted runtime helpers. The prefix is
'                      applied automatically by tiraLabelCreateNew$ while isCompilingRT is set;
'                      runtime emitters do not have to request it, and direct fixed @ labels remain valid.
'  %   (percent)     : User-defined labels (e.g., %10, %MY_LABEL). The parser applies this
'                      prefix automatically to isolate source labels from all internal @ labels.
'  #   (hash)        : Absolute symbol-index reference (e.g., #42). This bypasses scope
'                      resolution and directly targets a variable by its internal ID.

' NOTE: SUBROUTINE AND FUNCTION ARGUMENT PASSING
' This compiler supports traditional QBasic pass-by-reference behavior for
' standard scalar variables using a safe copy-in, copy-out mechanism
' When a variable is passed into a sub or function, a disconnected clone
' of that data is handed to the local scope. Upon returning, the compiler
' automatically copies the modified value back into the caller's original variable
' To enforce strict pass-by-value behavior similar to C++, the user can activate
' the #NOREF directive to completely disable this copy-out phase
' Arrays serve as a structural exception to this rule
' When an array is passed, its underlying heap memory address is assigned
' to the target parameter along with its corresponding bounds tracking
' Because both scopes point to the same memory location, modifications made
' to array elements inside the subroutine will reflect in the caller scope
' instantly regardless of whether the #NOREF directive is active

' NOTE: LEFT-SIDE IMPLICIT ASSUMPTION
' The compiler allows implicit variable creation on the left side of
' an assignment because it is processed first using the symbol resolver
' However, variables on the right hand side (RHS) of the assignment in most cases explicitly
' disable implicit resolution to prevent compiling undefined variables

' NOTE: TIRA AND THE INTELLIGENT MACRO-ASSEMBLER
' The compiler intermediate representation (TIRA) operates as a hybrid
' intelligent macro-assembler rather than a strictly typed IR
' The frontend stream-based parsers handle structural evaluation, operator precedence,
' and scope resolution without worrying about hardware-level type casting
' When TIRA commands are queued, the backend tasm_ suite acts as an intelligent
' agent to evaluate the symbol table and dynamically inject the correct x64
' hardware coercion instructions to resolve type mismatches natively
' This decoupling prevents redundant casting loops and keeps the frontend clean

' NOTE: X64 ABI COMPLIANCE AND STACK FRAMES
'
' The backend strictly adheres to the Windows x64 calling convention using
' stable RBP frame pointers to anchor the local execution scope
' When TC_ABI_PROLOGUE executes, the four volatile parameter registers
' (RCX, RDX, R8, R9) are safely spilled into the 32-byte shadow space
' provided by the caller
' TC_ABI_READ_ARG leverages this stable RBP anchor to retrieve all arguments
' sequentially from the stack, immunizing parameter retrieval against
' runtime RSP shifts or register clobbering
' Furthermore, TC_CALL universally guarantees a compliant 32-byte
' shadow space allocation for all function calls, internal or external
'
' NOTE: TIRA GLOBAL VARIABLE NAMING CONVENTION (THE HYBRID MODEL)
'
' THIS IS NOT FOR USER GLOBALS, ONLY COMPILER GLOBALS
'
' To prevent variable collisions and maintain strict hardware-level context
' across the TIRA backend, internal global variables are divided into two categories:
'
'  !G_ : Engine Globals. Core infrastructure and hardware state initialized at startup
'        (e.g., !G_WND_VAL_HWND, !G_MEM_PTR_PROCESS_HEAP).
'  !C_ : Compiler-Generated Constructs. Logic variables created dynamically by the parser
'        (e.g., literal pools, hoisted intrinsic containers, control block trackers).
'
' Format: ![G/C]_[SUBSYSTEM]_[ROLE]_[NAME]
'
' Goal: Piece 1 SUBSYSTEM answers which architectural engine module owns the data, slot 1
' The [SUBSYSTEM] prefix indicates where the variable belongs:
' - !G_SYS_    : Core compiler mechanics and critical state
' - !G_MEM_    : Heap allocation and memory tracking
' - !G_FLS_    : OS file system operations and handle tracking
' - !G_GFX_    : Graphical framebuffer and rendering state
' - !G_INO_    : Input/Output operations and buffers
' - !G_WND_    : Thread-safe variables for the Windows Message Loop
' - !G_SND_    : Sound generation and aud io state variables
' - !G_ERR_    : Exception handling, stack restoration, and error state tracking
' - !C_EVL_    : Permanent storage for Shunting Yard intrinsic evaluations.
' - !C_PRS_    : Storage for parser variables and literal extraction
'                 (When intrinsic functions are evaluated/migrated, the term "HOIS"
'                 is explicitly integrated here in the descriptive portion, e.g., !C_EVL_VAL_HOISASC1)
' - !C_SYS_    : Dynamic code generation logic for structures like FOR/NEXT or SELECT CASE
' - !C_OTH_    : Fallback or miscellaneous compiler tracking state variables (e.g., argument staging)

' Goal: Piece 2 ROLE answers how the x64 hardware physically interfaces with the data, slot 2
' The [ROLE] slot is strictly 3 characters and dictates exactly how the variable is used:
' - PTR_ : A 64-bit memory address (used strictly with TC_READ_MEM / TC_WRITE_MEM)
' - IDX_ : An array index, offset multiplier, or tracking counter
' - VAL_ : A raw scalar numeric value (Defaults to 64-bit integer)
' - V32_ : An explicit 32-bit signed integer value
' - DSC_ : A 24-byte String Descriptor containing a pointer, length, and flag
' - FLG_ : A boolean state, spinlock, or toggle
' - ARR_ : A fixed block of memory allocated as an array in the data segment
' - UDT_ : User data type. Implementation in process

' Goal: Piece 3 NAME answers what specific semantic object or feature is being tracked, slot 3
' The [NAME] segment uniquely identifies the variable. It can be of flexible length,
' accommodating any level of descriptive detail without breaking the fixed-width prefixes. Valid examples:
' - FBFR      : Raw hardware pixel framebuffers (e.g., !G_GFX_PTR_FRMB_BACK)
' - CUR       : Cursor position tracking (e.g., !G_GFX_VAL_CUR_X)
' - LTR_      : Static hardcoded literal values (e.g., !C_EVL_DSC_LTRL_1)
' - HOIS      : Extracted intrinsic evaluations (e.g., !C_EVL_VAL_HOISASC1)
' - TEMP      : Temporary memory boundaries (e.g., !G_MEM_PTR_TEMP_HEAP)
' - KBD       : Keyboard tracking arrays and state (e.g., !G_INO_VAL_KBD_HEAD)
' - MOUSE     : Mouse coordinate and button states (e.g., !G_INO_VAL_MOUSE_X)
' - BUF       : Application logic and background queues (e.g., !G_GFX_ARR_BUFR_FRONT)
' - PLOT      : Draw operation coordinate math (e.g., !G_GFX_PTR_PLOT_TARGET)
' - VIEW      : Active viewport boundaries (e.g., !G_GFX_VAL_VIEW_X1)
' - MSG       : Thread message loop parameters (e.g., !G_WND_PTR_MSG_LOOP)
' - DATA      : READ/DATA pointer tracking (e.g., !G_SYS_VAL_DATAINDEX)
' - FMT       : Fixed C-style formatting strings (e.g., !G_INO_DSC_FMT_G_DBL)
'
' No descriptive term should match an allowed slot 1 or 2 term. Underscores have limited allowance in slot 3,
' however they are discouraged, and the LLM should avoid adding new underscores to slot 3 descriptions
' The first two characters or slot 3 may not be _

' NOTE: TIRA RUNTIME TEMPORARY NAMING CONVENTION (!R_ PREFIX)
'
' The !R_ prefix is dedicated strictly to ephemeral, dynamically generated
' scratchpad variables used natively within internal Runtime Helpers (e.g., RT_LINE).
' Because Runtime Helpers and user SUB/FUNCTION blocks both use the tiraDimVar$
' mechanism to allocate temporary memory on the local execution stack frame,
' the !R_ prefix acts as a strict architectural sandbox. It completely isolates
' runtime execution state from user code, preventing catastrophic namespace
' and stack-offset collisions during compilation.
'
' Format: !R_[TYPE]_[BASENAME]_[COUNTER]
'
' Goal: Piece 1 [TYPE] identifies the hardware data size and nature, slot 2
' Driven directly by the compiler's internal TYPE_ constants, tiraDimVar$ injects a
' strict 4-character type identifier:
' - TI64 : 64-bit integer
' - TU64 : Unsigned 64-bit integer
' - TLNG : 32-bit signed long
' - TSNG : 32-bit single-precision float
' - TDBL : 64-bit double-precision float
' - TSTR : 24-byte string descriptor
' - TBYT : 8-bit byte
' - TUDT : User-defined type
'
' Goal: Piece 2 [BASENAME] identifies what the variable is doing, slot 3
' A descriptive semantic identifier passed into the baseName$ argument of tiraDimVar$.
' Valid examples:
' - ARG1     : Holds the first incoming ABI parameter
' - X_VAL    : Holds a coordinate or spatial calculation
' - PTR      : Holds a memory address or offset
'
' Goal: Piece 3 [COUNTER] guarantees collision safety, slot 4
' A unique deterministic integer suffix tracked by t.TempCounter. It resets per
' block to ensure Static Single Assignment (SSA) safety across the compilation pass.
'
' Valid Examples:
' !R_TI64_ARG1_0 (A temporary 64-bit integer holding Argument 1)
' !R_TSTR_VAL_1  (A temporary dynamic string descriptor)



' NOTE: AVOIDANCE OF LEGACY TYPE SUFFIXES ON TIRA VARIABLES
' Internal TIRA variables strictly avoid using legacy QB-style type suffixes
' (such as #, !, %, &) at the end of their names to keep the [ROLE] suffixes pristine
' If an internal variable requires a specific hardware type (such as TYPE_DOUBLE),
' the compiler resolves the clean name and explicitly overrides the .DataType property
' in the symbol table immediately afterward
' Strings (_DSC) remain the sole exception, as the standard string signifier
' is intentionally integrated directly into the role designation
